A floating-point operand of '&&', '||' or '!' is never compared against zero.
The truth test is emitted as an integer 'test %rax,%rax' after the operand has
been loaded into an SSE register, so the branch is decided by whatever was left
in %rax and every such expression answers as though the operand were true -
'and_dd(2.5, 0.0)' returns 1, and so does 'and_dd(0.0, 0.0)'. There is no
comisd/ucomisd anywhere in the generated function.

For '&&' and '||' the fix belongs in the legacy backend
(src/x86_64/codegen_x86_64.c), where the truth test for a logical operand is
generated. Those are not IR-pipeline bugs: instruction selection does not cover
them, so canEmitMachineFunction() declines and they fall back - a file
containing only 'int f(double a, double b) { return a && b; }' compiles to a
byte-identical object with and without '-experimental'.

'!' on a double is different: the new backend does emit it, and it fails there
in two further places. Selection picks 'test' with an SSE operand
('test.8 $xmm8, $xmm8' - not an instruction that exists), and emission then
encodes the xmm operand as the same-numbered *general-purpose* register, so the
bytes read 'test %r8,%r8' and the following 'sete' consumes unrelated flags.
The second of those is the one with reach beyond floats: nothing validates an
operand's register class on the way out of stage 3, so anything arriving in the
wrong bank is silently encoded as a different real register instead of being
refused. checkemit's differential against objdump is what surfaced it - the
machine IR says xmm, the bytes say r8.

Narrowing it: 'if (a)' on a double is correct today, and so is an explicit
'a != 0.0'. Both are covered as controls in the fixture. So the float branch
machinery exists and works; what is missing is its use when the float is an
operand of a logical operator rather than the controlling expression of an
'if'. The '&&'/'||' translation in the IR pipeline already does the right thing
here (src/ir/ast2ir.c, translateLogicalExpression, which picks IR_E_FNE for a
real-typed operand) - it just never gets the chance.

Which check fails first depends on the stale register contents and therefore on
the caller: the fixture returns 1 compiled normally and 6 under
'-experimental'. Both are stable per build. If this ever reports as passing,
confirm a comisd against zero in the disassembly before deleting this marker -
favourable garbage would look identical from the exit code alone.

See docs/ir-codegen-design.md section 10.
